equested changes and save the file.\n\n<tool_call>\nname: write_file\narguments: {\"path\": \"notes.md\", \"contents\": \"# My Document\\n\\n## Section 1\\n\\nContent here.\\n\\n## Section 2\\n\\nMore content.\\n\\n## Section 3\\n\\nNew section content.\\n\"}\n</tool_call>"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "File written successfully.", "tool_call_id": "call_1865fbc5c4ce"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Done."}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Your job is to edit files using tools exactly as the user instructs.\n\nYou have access to these tools:\n- `read_file(path)`: Read the contents of a file at the given path\n- `write_file(path, contents)`: Write contents to a file at the given path\n\nCRITICAL GUIDELINES:\n- Do NOT infer or generate content based on file extensions\n- Do NOT create new content that wasn't in the original file\n- Do NOT \"fix\" or \"improve\" the file content\n- Do NOT add domain-specific logic or code\n- ONLY perform the exact text manipulation the user requests\n- If the user says \"replace X with Y\", ONLY replace X with Y - nothing else\n- If the user says \"add the content 'X'\", add ONLY 'X' - nothing else\n- If the file is EMPTY and user says \"add content 'X'\", the file should contain ONLY 'X' (no other content)\n- Do NOT add example content like \"line1: first line\" or \"existing content\" unless it's already in the file\n- Content to add/remove is usually specified in single or double quotes: 'text' or \"text\"\n- Extract the exact content from quotes - do not modify or interpret it\n- When reading a file, use ONLY the content that's actually in the file (from read_file tool response)\n- Do NOT infer or add content that wasn't in the file you read\n- Preserve all other content exactly as it was (if any exists)\n- Follow the user's instructions precisely and literally\n- Use tools to read, modify, and write files"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit the file at `config.yaml`.\n\nReplace the word 'stuff' with 'blah'"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "I'll read the file, make the requested changes, and save it.\n\n<tool_call>\nname: read_file\narguments: {\"path\": \"config.yaml\"}\n</tool_call>"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "package main\n\nimport \"fmt\"\n\nfunc main() {\n    fmt.Println(\"Hello, stuff.\")\n    fmt.Println(\"This is a test.\")\n    fmt.Println(\"Another line.\")\n}", "tool_call_id": "call_57a37e728e98"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Now I'll make the requested changes and save the file.\n\n<tool_call>\nname: write_file\narguments: {\"path\": \"config.yaml\", \"contents\": \"package main\\n\\nimport \\\"fmt\\\"\\n\\nfunc main() {\\n    fmt.Println(\\\"Hello, blah.\\\")\\n    fmt.Println(\\\"This is a test.\\\")\\n    fmt.Println(\\\"Another line.\\\")\\n}\"}\n</tool_call>"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "File written successfully.", "tool_call_id": "call_a368c6303ecd"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Done."}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Your job is to edit files using tools exactly as the user instructs.\n\nYou have access to these tools:\n- `read_file(path)`: Read the contents of a file at the given path\n- `write_file(path, contents)`: Write contents to a file at the given path\n\nCRITICAL GUIDELINES:\n- Do NOT infer or generate content based on file extensions\n- Do NOT create new content that wasn't in the original file\n- Do NOT \"fix\" or \"improve\" the file content\n- Do NOT add domain-specific logic or code\n- ONLY perform the exact text manipulation the user requests\n- If the user says \"replace X with Y\", ONLY replace X with Y - nothing else\n- If the user says \"add the content 'X'\", add ONLY 'X' - nothing else\n- If the file is EMPTY and user says \"add content 'X'\", the file should contain ONLY 'X' (no other content)\n- Do NOT add example content like \"line1: first line\" or \"existing content\" unless it's already in the file\n- Content to add/remove is usually specified in single or double quotes: 'text' or \"text\"\n- Extract the exact content from quotes - do not modify or interpret it\n- When reading a file, use ONLY the content that's actually in the file (from read_file tool response)\n- Do NOT infer or add content that wasn't in the file you read\n- Preserve all other content exactly as it was (if any exists)\n- Follow the user's instructions precisely and literally\n- Use tools to read, modify, and write files"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit the file at `input.txt`.\n\nInsert '    New indented line' after line 2, preserving indentation"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "I'll read the file, make the requested changes, and save it.\n\n<tool_call>\nname: read_file\narguments: {\"path\": \"input.txt\"}\n</tool_call>"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "    Indented line 1\n    Indented line 2\n    \n    Blank line above\n    Indented line 3", "tool_call_id": "call_677a44dd2d98"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Now I'll make the requested changes and save the file.\n\n<tool_call>\nname: write_file\narguments: {\"path\": \"input.txt\", \"contents\": \"    Indented line 1\\n    Indented line 2\\n    New indented line\\n    Blank line above\\n    Indented line 3\"}\n</tool_call>"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "File written successfully.", "tool_call_id": "call_30fb53038c58"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Done."}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Your job is to create and edit files using tools exactly as the user instructs.\n\nYou have access to these tools:\n- `read_file(path)`: Read the contents of a file at the given path\n- `write_file(path, contents)`: Write contents to a file at the given path\n\nGuidelines:\n- Do not infer missing logic\n- Do not create domain-specific content\n- Only create the content the user describes\n- Follow the user's instructions precisely\n- If a file doesn't exist, use write_file to create it"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Create a new file at `example.txt` with the following content:\n\n# My Document\n\nThis is a new document.\n\n## Section 1\n\nContent here.\n\n## Section 2\n\nMore content.\n"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "I'll create the new file with the specified content.\n\n<tool_call>\nname: write_file\narguments: {\"path\": \"example.txt\", \"contents\": \"# My Document\\n\\nThis is a new document.\\n\\n## Section 1\\n\\nContent here.\\n\\n## Section 2\\n\\nMore content.\\n\"}\n</tool_call>"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "File written successfully.", "tool_call_id": "call_a2810de57c45"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Done. The file has been created."}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Your job is to edit files using tools exactly as the user instructs.\n\nYou have access to these tools:\n- `read_file(path)`: Read the contents of a file at the given path\n- `write_file(path, contents)`: Write contents to a file at the given path\n\nCRITICAL GUIDELINES:\n- Do NOT infer or generate content based on file extensions\n- Do NOT create new content that wasn't in the original file\n- Do NOT \"fix\" or \"improve\" the file content\n- Do NOT add domain-specific logic or code\n- ONLY perform the exact text manipulation the user requests\n- If the user says \"replace X with Y\", ONLY replace X with Y - nothing else\n- If the user says \"add the content 'X'\", add ONLY 'X' - nothing else\n- If the file is EMPTY and user says \"add content 'X'\", the file should contain ONLY 'X' (no other content)\n- Do NOT add example content like \"line1: first line\" or \"existing content\" unless it's already in the file\n- Content to add/remove is usually specified in single or double quotes: 'text' or \"text\"\n- Extract the exact content from quotes - do not modify or interpret it\n- When reading a file, use ONLY the content that's actually in the file (from read_file tool response)\n- Do NOT infer or add content that wasn't in the file you read\n- Preserve all other content exactly as it was (if any exists)\n- Follow the user's instructions precisely and literally\n- Use tools to read, modify, and write files"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit the file at `handler.go`.\n\nAdd error handling to the processData function: return an error if data is empty, and update main to handle the error"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "I'll read the file, make the requested changes, and save it.\n\n<tool_call>\nname: read_file\narguments: {\"path\": \"handler.go\"}\n</tool_call>"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "package main\n\nimport \"fmt\"\n\nfunc processData(data string) {\n\tfmt.Println(\"Processing:\", data)\n}\n\nfunc main() {\n\tprocessData(\"test\")\n}\n", "tool_call_id": "call_e6e57e5817b9"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Now I'll make the requested changes and save the file.\n\n<tool_call>\nname: write_file\narguments: {\"path\": \"handler.go\", \"contents\": \"package main\\n\\nimport \\\"fmt\\\"\\n\\nfunc processData(data string) error {\\n\\tif data == \\\"\\\" {\\n\\t\\treturn fmt.Errorf(\\\"data cannot be empty\\\")\\n\\t}\\n\\tfmt.Println(\\\"Processing:\\\", data)\\n\\treturn nil\\n}\\n\\nfunc main() {\\n\\tif err := processData(\\\"test\\\"); err != nil {\\n\\t\\tfmt.Println(\\\"Error:\\\", err)\\n\\t}\\n}\\n\"}\n</tool_call>"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "File written successfully.", "tool_call_id": "call_38a2c280400e"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Done."}]}
